  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\n19Z water vapor imagery showed a shortwave lifting across the \nforecast area within a broader upper trough over much of the western \nU.S. Surface obs showed low pressure along the high plains with high \npressure to the east of the Great Plains allowing southerly low level\nflow to continue to bring moisture north. \n\nFor the rest of this afternoon, the light rain associated with the \nshortwave should exit the forecast area as the wave moves northeast \nof the area. General subsidence behind this wave is forecast to \npreclude any redevelopment across north central Kansas. However the \nsoutherly low level flow is expected to maintain some isentropic \nlift tonight and the CAMs continue to show scattered showers \ndeveloping towards midnight. Forecast soundings suggest there will \nbe some modest elevated instability around 500 J/kg. But bulk shear \nshould be weak (around 20KT) and effective shear for an elevated \nparcel even weaker. The forecast has some chance POPs across \nnortheast and east central Kansas expecting coverage for the most \npart to be scattered. \n\nShortwave ridging is progged to develop overhead for Sunday and \nMonday. Some of the deterministic solutions have isolated QPF progs \nSunday afternoon and overnight into Monday morning. Am not sure what \nis causing this since there isn't much synoptic support nor lift on \nthe isentropic charts. With this in mind have kept POPs less than 20 \npercent. There should be scattered cloud cover with no strong temp \nadvection for Sunday and Monday. This should allow temps to persist \nin the upper 70s for highs and lower 60s for lows. \n\nThe main focus of the forecast remains on the extended period of wet \nweather for Tuesday through Friday. Deterministic solutions are \nconverging on a closed upper low slowly trekking from the \nsouthwestern U.S. across the plains. And there is a trend in the \nensemble solutions to this idea as well. So forecast confidence is \nimproving. Initially the remnants of Polo are progged to be absorbed \ninto the upper trough with a good fetch of tropical moisture \nadvecting into the central plains for Tuesday and Tuesday night. \nEnsembles continue to show moisture forecast to be in the atmosphere \nas 2 to 3 standard deviations above normal. And operational \nsolutions show PWs around 2 inches. As this moisture advects into \nthe central plains Tuesday and Tuesday night, this represents first \nopportunity for heavy rainfall to impact the area. The second \nopportunity is with cyclogenesis progged to occur Wednesday night \nand Thursday. The 12Z GFS and ECMWF tend to keep the surface low to \nthe southwest and south of the forecast area through Thursday night \nplacing the forecast area within a favorable synoptic location for \ngood moisture advection and upper level divergence to allow the \nregeneration of precipitation. By this time the available moisture \nshould have shifted to the east, but soils could be saturated from \nearlier precipitation. As for rain totals, the NBM has the \nprobability for 1 to 3 inches or rain in the 60 to 75 percent \nrange. Chances for greater than 3 inches sit around 50 percent. \nWorse case scenarios are pointing to rain totals of 3 to 5 inches \nwhich could lead to flooding. Eventually the upper low should get \nkicked east with dry forecast for Saturday.",
